dialect grammar
grammar stdio.stl.snapshot
extension stl
start document

# FG1: real ASCII STL (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/STL_(file_format)) grammar — replaces the old
# one-line-header ABNF placeholder (`dialect grammar stdio.stl.snapshot` -- 2 tokens on one line,
# rejected by this dialect's parser -- plus `[SP name]`/`1*(ALPHA / DIGIT ...)`/`NL` bodies outside
# this dialect's alphabet entirely). Models `decode_stl_ascii`/`encode_stl_ascii` exactly
# (⚙️engine/component.rs:20-89, cited verbatim in W0's own recon row for this standard). Envelope
# preamble line + its trailing newline are stripped before this grammar runs
# (store::semio_format::split_text_preamble) — `document` starts with the bare envelope-id token,
# matching the m5 harness's own reconstructed body shape (`dsl_body_from_fixture`,
# `🧪️fixture/🦀️-sweep.rs`) and json/csv/binary's own pilot precedent exactly.
#
# `solid`/`endsolid`'s name token is genuinely "rest of physical line, arbitrary text" — W0's own
# documented gap for this exact standard (shared with obj's `o`/`g`), confirmed real by
# `encode_stl_ascii`:77/87, which writes `solid_name` completely unescaped, no length limit, no
# character restriction. M1's `LINE` raw-span terminal is this dialect's own worked citation for
# THIS EXACT case (`📖️grammar/🦀️.rs`'s `RawSpanEnd` doc comment literally reads: "`LINE`
# -- rest of the current physical line ... obj's `o`/`g` names, stl's `solid <name>`/`endsolid
# <name>`, dxf's opaque group-code value lines") — the honest primitive this artifact's W0 row
# asked for, not a lossy re-join. Whitespace/newlines are lexer trivia stripped before recognition
# (`Recognizer::recognize`), so no `ws`/`NL` production is needed anywhere below.
artifact-mark = "stdio.stl"
document = artifact-mark solid

solid = "solid" LINE facet* "endsolid" LINE
facet = "facet" "normal" vec3 "outer" "loop" vertex vertex vertex "endloop" "endfacet"
vertex = "vertex" vec3
vec3 = number number number

# `f64` coordinates print via Rust's `Display` (`format!("{nx} {ny} {nz}")`), which never emits a
# trailing `.0` for a whole-numbered float (`format!("{}", 0.0_f64)` == `"0"`) — so a coordinate
# lexes as INT whenever its value happens to be integral, FLOAT otherwise. Same `INT | FLOAT` idiom
# json's own `number` production uses, for the identical reason.
number = INT | FLOAT
